-- title:
--   The prime q is irreducible in ℤ_{(q)}
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a natural number and assume $q$ is prime. Consider the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt q`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of those rationals whose denominator (in lowest terms, as a natural number) is coprime to $q$; this is the localisation $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$ of $\mathbb{Z}$ away from $q$. The assertion is that the image of the natural number $q$ under the canonical ring map $\mathbb{N} \to$ [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt q`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) is an irreducible element of that ring, i.e. it is neither a unit nor zero-divisor-trivial in the sense of Mathlib's `Irreducible`: it is not a unit of $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$, and whenever $q = ab$ with $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$, at least one of $a$, $b$ is a unit of $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$. Since $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$ is a discrete valuation ring, this says exactly that $q$ is a uniformiser of $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$, its maximal ideal being generated by $q$.
--
--   This is the elementary statement that a prime $q$ generates the maximal ideal of $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$, i.e. is a uniformiser there. It is the input required wherever a uniformiser of the coefficient ring $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$ must be exhibited, for instance in the Hopf-algebra and Dieudonné-module arguments about finite flat group schemes and in the integral-model computations over $\mathbb{Z}_{(q)}$ that cite it.
